  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 145 PM CDT Wed Jul 29 2026\n\nFor this afternoon, a weakening MCV, evident in the diminishing\nradar returns, is slowly tracking to the southeast. This may \nbring spotty showers and storms to eastern KS and western MO for\nthe remainder of the afternoon, but it seems likely that the \ndiminishing trend will continue with most areas staying dry to \nonly seeing a trace of precipitation. Otherwise, overnight and \ninto tomorrow morning, another area of showers and storms looks \npossible as the overall setup is similar. In a very moist \natmosphere, with PWAT values near/just above 2\" in eastern KS \nand western MO, at the interface of the surface high to the \neast, and weak mid-level flow aloft, an area of weak isentropic \nascent sets up. This should provide just enough ascent to lead \nto precipitation development. This may be aided by any upstream \nconvection and potential MCV that isn't readily apparent in any\nmodel depiction yet, or even generated yet. The RAP does hint \nat some of this potential with some internal PV noted from \n600-400mb. Such is forecasting on the east side of the upper \nridge within weak/modest northwesterly flow. \n\nA stronger shortwave trough will track across the northern edges of \nthe upper ridge Thursday evening and overnight. Forcing \nassociated with this shortwave should lead to an area of storms \ndeveloping to our northwest, that then tracks southeastward with\ntime, affecting the forecast area Thursday night to Friday \nmorning. Models show modest instability and shear into the area \nthrough the overnight hours. Given this, and the propensity for \nnorthwest flow convective systems to track further southeast \nthan anticipated, a few strong to marginally severe storms look \npossible for northeastern KS and northwestern MO, in this \npattern. \n\nIn the wake of this system cooler and much drier air will settle \ninto the area. Dewpoints in the low to mid 60s Saturday and Sunday \nwill be a welcomed relief compared to the low to mid 80 degree \ndewpoints we experienced last weekend.",
